  "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-0.8)=-0.927\\,295\u2026\u2248-0.9273\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-0.9273$ is not in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-0.9273.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-0.9273+2\\pi \u22485.3559\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/942d3d727557ee280b93b212e856f4a696bcf06438944299fd6596dfb5b147f4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.9273 \\\\  & \u22484.0689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are  \n\n$$\nx\u22484.1,\\,x\u22485.4.\n$$",
